.TH STTY 1 "" "" "mcos Manual"
.SH NAME
stty \- show or change the terminal's line settings
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B stty
.br
.B stty
.IR setting ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B stty
reads and changes the line settings (termios flags) of the tty on its
standard input, as Linux's stty does. With no arguments it lists every
flag on one line, sorted, a flag that is off with a
.B \-
before it.
.PP
Each
.I setting
is applied in order; the flags are written back to the tty once all have
been read. An invalid setting stops it before anything is changed.
.SH SETTINGS
.TP
.I flag
Turns
.I flag
on.
.TP
.BI \- flag
Turns
.I flag
off.
.TP
.BR raw " or " \-cooked
Turns off
.BR icanon ,
.BR echo ,
.B isig
and
.BR icrnl .
.TP
.BR \-raw " or " cooked
Turns those four back on.
.TP
.B sane
Puts every flag below back on (Linux's defaults).
.TP
.B size
Prints the terminal's rows and columns, as two numbers on a line.
.SH FLAGS
.TP
.B icanon
Line editing: input is read a line at a time, with Backspace, ^U and ^D.
.TP
.B echo
Typed characters are shown.
.TP
.B echoe
Backspace erases the character on the screen.
.TP
.B echoctl
Control characters are echoed as
.BR ^X .
.TP
.B isig
^C sends SIGINT to the terminal's foreground process group.
.TP
.B icrnl
Enter (a carriage return) comes in as a new line.
.TP
.B onlcr
A new line goes out as carriage return and new line.
.TP
.B iutf8
Backspace erases a whole UTF-8 character.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success; 1 when standard input isn't a tty, a setting is invalid, or
the settings can't be set.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
stty \-echo
stty raw
stty sane
stty size
.fi
.SH NOTES
These are the tty's settings, kept by the kernel. A terminal's own modes
(cursor keys, the alternate screen) are set with escape sequences, not
with
.BR stty .
There are no special-character settings (such as
.BR "intr ^C" )
and no speeds.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sh (1),
.BR clear (1)
